{
  "schema": "la-module@1",
  "module_key": "2024-01-19-questions-help",
  "kind": "guide",
  "status": "written",
  "title": "The Power to Ask Anything",
  "description": "Any statement can be turned into a question using these three methods.",
  "can_do": [
    "Turn any sentence into a question with {est-ce que}.",
    "Recognize a question made with a rising voice or a flipped verb, and know where each one fits."
  ],
  "minutes": 5,
  "line": "Three ways to turn any sentence into a question, and when to use each one.",
  "parts": [
    {
      "id": "p-three-ways-to-ask",
      "heading": "Three ways to ask",
      "text": [
        "Every French question uses one of three methods."
      ],
      "note": "Start with {est-ce que}. It works for every question and changes nothing else in the sentence.",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "ways": [
            {
              "n": 1,
              "fr": "Est-ce que",
              "t": "Works for every question",
              "d": "Anywhere"
            },
            {
              "n": 2,
              "fr": "",
              "t": "A rising voice",
              "d": "With friends and family"
            },
            {
              "n": 3,
              "fr": "",
              "t": "Inversion: the verb first",
              "d": "Formal speech and writing"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "p-est-ce-que",
      "heading": "{Est-ce que}",
      "text": [
        "{Est-ce que} means roughly “is it that”. Put it in front of any statement and the statement becomes a question."
      ],
      "blocks": [
        {
          "build": [
            {
              "k": "A statement",
              "fr": "Tu as un chat",
              "en": "You have a cat"
            },
            {
              "k": "Add",
              "fr": "Est-ce que",
              "en": "is it that"
            },
            {
              "k": "A question",
              "fr": "Est-ce que tu as un chat ?",
              "en": "Do you have a cat?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "lines": [
            [
              "Où est-ce que tu es ?",
              "Where are you?"
            ],
            [
              "Quand est-ce que tu viens ?",
              "When are you coming?"
            ]
          ],
          "heading": "With a question word in front"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "p-raise-your-voice",
      "heading": "Raise your voice",
      "text": [
        "In everyday speech, French speakers often keep the statement as it is and raise their voice at the end."
      ],
      "note": "Same words, different tone. This is how friends talk to each other.",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "rise": [
            {
              "fr": "Tu viens ce soir.",
              "en": "You are coming tonight.",
              "tone": "flat"
            },
            {
              "fr": "Tu viens ce soir ?",
              "en": "Are you coming tonight?",
              "tone": "up"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "p-flip-the-verb",
      "heading": "Flip the verb",
      "text": [
        "For formal speech and writing, put the verb before the subject and join them with a hyphen. This is called inversion."
      ],
      "note": "You will see it in books, formal emails and the news.",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "flip": [
            {
              "fr": "Tu es prêt.",
              "en": "You are ready.",
              "parts": [
                [
                  "Tu",
                  "s"
                ],
                [
                  "es",
                  "v"
                ],
                [
                  "prêt",
                  ""
                ]
              ]
            },
            {
              "fr": "Es-tu prêt ?",
              "en": "Are you ready?",
              "parts": [
                [
                  "Es",
                  "v"
                ],
                [
                  "-",
                  "h"
                ],
                [
                  "tu",
                  "s"
                ],
                [
                  "prêt ?",
                  ""
                ]
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "p-which-one-to-use",
      "heading": "Which one to use",
      "text": [],
      "note": "Use {est-ce que} for everything at first. The other two come as you hear more French.",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "ways": [
            {
              "n": 1,
              "fr": "Est-ce que",
              "t": "Use it anywhere",
              "d": "Clear, and always correct"
            },
            {
              "n": 2,
              "fr": "",
              "t": "A rising voice",
              "d": "Relaxed, for friends and family"
            },
            {
              "n": 3,
              "fr": "",
              "t": "Inversion",
              "d": "Formal, for writing and speeches"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "checks": [
    {
      "id": "k1",
      "ask": "Which one turns {Tu as un chat} into a question?",
      "options": [
        {
          "text": "Est-ce que tu as un chat ?",
          "fr": true,
          "right": true
        },
        {
          "text": "Tu est-ce que as un chat ?",
          "fr": true
        },
        {
          "text": "Tu as un chat est-ce que ?",
          "fr": true
        }
      ],
      "why": "{Est-ce que} goes in front, and the rest of the sentence stays the same."
    },
    {
      "id": "k2",
      "ask": "Listen. How is this question made?",
      "say": "Tu viens ce soir ?",
      "show": "Tu viens ce soir ?",
      "options": [
        {
          "text": "With est-ce que"
        },
        {
          "text": "With a rising voice",
          "right": true
        },
        {
          "text": "By flipping the verb"
        }
      ],
      "why": "The words are the same as the statement. The voice rises at the end."
    },
    {
      "id": "k3",
      "ask": "Which one is the formal question?",
      "options": [
        {
          "text": "Tu es prêt ?",
          "fr": true
        },
        {
          "text": "Es-tu prêt ?",
          "fr": true,
          "right": true
        },
        {
          "text": "Est-ce que tu es prêt ?",
          "fr": true
        }
      ],
      "why": "The verb comes first, joined to {tu} with a hyphen: {Es-tu prêt ?}"
    }
  ]
}
